00:26:59,120 --> 00:27:13,120
And you can see now as well that the
Euro-dollar futures contract linked to the
three-month LIBOR, linked to three-month
US Treasuries, linked to the official base
rate in the United States.

160
00:27:14,120 --> 00:27:36,120
You can see how all of this is connected
and how the rest of the world depends
extremely heavily on the US dollar and the
liquidity and health of the US Treasury
and bond market and also US yields and
rates.

161
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:46,120
You know, it is often stated that when the
US sleazes, the rest of the world catches
a cold.

162
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:50,120
This is very true in the bond markets.

163
00:27:51,120 --> 00:28:02,120
When liquidity is abundant in US bond
markets, the rest of the world has
liquidity.

164
00:28:04,120 --> 00:28:12,120
When liquidity dries up in the United
States, the rest of the world is in
trouble.

165
00:28:13,120 --> 00:28:22,120
And this is why it's so important to
obtain a macroeconomic view, not just of
the US, but of the rest of the world.

166
00:28:23,120 --> 00:28:29,120
This is why it's so important to
understand what is going on in US bond
markets.

167
00:28:30,120 --> 00:28:32,120
But what about issuers and buyers of
bonds?

168
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:33,120
Who are they?

169
00:28:34,120 --> 00:28:46,120
Well, the issuer, the definition of issuer
in the United States, for example, for a
government bond, is the United States
Treasury.

170
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:54,120
They are financing their spending or
outgoings of the US government.

171
00:28:55,120 --> 00:28:58,120
So the US Treasury issues a government
bond.

172
00:28:59,120 --> 00:29:05,120
And the private market invests or lends
that money to the US government.

173
00:29:06,120 --> 00:29:12,120
And the US government is using that money
to finance government spending.

174
00:29:13,120 --> 00:29:21,120
Now, the buyers of government bonds, the
entities that are investing or lending
money to the US government.

175
00:29:22,120 --> 00:29:34,120
The buyers of government bonds are pension
funds, mutual funds, hedge funds,
investment banks, commercial banks, even
individuals and even other countries.

176
00:29:35,120 --> 00:29:36,120
Central banks.

177
00:29:36,120 --> 00:29:37,120
Central banks.

178
00:29:38,120 --> 00:29:39,120
And they are traded.

179
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:45,120
Debt securities trade over the counter, or
in short, OTC.

180
00:29:46,120 --> 00:29:54,120
They're not like equities, where equities,
stocks and stock instruments trade on
exchange.

181
00:29:55,120 --> 00:30:02,120
Bond futures, like Euro dollars, futures
contracts, do, however, trade on exchange.

182
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:19,120
And it's important to understand these
dynamics because as traders and portfolio
managers, when we're seeking information
from the bond markets for the outlook of
economic growth and liquidity.

183
00:30:20,120 --> 00:30:24,120
What we need to really understand is money
flows.

184
00:30:26,120 --> 00:30:33,120
And money flows are very, very important
in the context of government bond markets.

185
00:30:34,120 --> 00:30:39,120
But let's have a look at the world's
largest and most liquid bond markets just
to understand this globally.

186
00:30:40,120 --> 00:30:44,120
The US Treasury market, by far the biggest
denominated in US dollars.

187
00:30:45,120 --> 00:30:49,120
The European government bond market
denominated in euros.

188
00:30:50,120 --> 00:30:54,120
UK gilts denominated in pounds, British
pounds.

189
00:30:55,120 --> 00:31:03,120
And the Japanese government bonds, JGBs,
that market denominated in yen, Japanese
yen.

190
00:31:04,120 --> 00:31:20,120
There's also peripheral markets globally,
such as Switzerland, Germany, Italy,
Canada, Australia, New Zealand and various
emerging market countries in regions like
Latin America, Asia, Eastern Europe and
Africa.

191
00:31:21,120 --> 00:31:26,120
Now, just to give you a sense of the size
of US bond markets.

192
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:31,120
US bond markets make up almost 40 trillion
dollars in value.

193
00:31:32,120 --> 00:31:41,120
And when we compare this to the stock
market, domestic stocks in the US
represent around 20 trillion dollars in
value.

194
00:31:42,120 --> 00:31:45,120
In terms of daily trading volume, so
liquidity.

195
00:31:46,120 --> 00:31:53,120
The daily trading volume in bonds in the
US is around 700 billion dollars per day.

196
00:31:54,120 --> 00:32:03,120
This is basically around three and a half
times the size of the stock market volumes
in the US.

197
00:32:04,120 --> 00:32:17,120
Clearly, market rates are a very important
factor and a very efficient indicator of
relative value for other financial
instruments.

198
00:32:18,120 --> 00:32:25,120
Now, if we're going to understand how the
bond market affects equities,

199
00:32:26,120 --> 00:32:34,120
we need to look at it in the context of a
business cycle, an interest rate cycle, a
credit cycle.

200
00:32:36,120 --> 00:32:38,120
Let's have a look at a typical cycle.

201
00:32:39,120 --> 00:32:41,120
Let's start from the very top.

202
00:32:43,120 --> 00:32:47,120
Where we look at the beginning of an
economic growth boom.

203
00:32:48,120 --> 00:32:49,120
What tends to happen?

204
00:32:49,120 --> 00:32:52,120
What actually happens when this occurs?

205
00:32:53,120 --> 00:32:55,120
Well, money becomes cheap.

206
00:32:56,120 --> 00:32:56,120
Okay.

207
00:32:57,120 --> 00:33:03,120
Money becomes low priced and we get an
increase in liquidity.

208
00:33:04,120 --> 00:33:05,120
Okay.

209
00:33:06,120 --> 00:33:07,120
Prices start to move.

210
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:15,120
The cost of money, the price of money
becomes relatively cheap or cheaper than
before.

211
00:33:16,120 --> 00:33:17,120
What happens?

212
00:33:17,120 --> 00:33:18,120
The economy starts moving.

213
00:33:19,120 --> 00:33:22,120
Governments, companies and individuals
borrow more of it.

214
00:33:23,120 --> 00:33:27,120
We enter a phase of high growth.

215
00:33:28,120 --> 00:33:33,120
So we get higher growth prospects, lots of
consumption and investment.

216
00:33:34,120 --> 00:33:38,120
What happens to equity market, stock
market valuations?

217
00:33:39,120 --> 00:33:44,120
We get higher equity market valuations and
higher earnings.

218
00:33:45,120 --> 00:33:46,120
And then what happens?

219
00:33:46,120 --> 00:33:54,120
In response to that, money becomes more
relatively expensive than before.

220
00:33:54,120 --> 00:33:56,120
Money becomes higher in price.

221
00:33:57,120 --> 00:33:58,120
It becomes more expensive.

222
00:33:59,120 --> 00:34:08,120
And then we have an end to the economic
cycle where the economy resets to an
equilibrium level.

223
00:34:09,120 --> 00:34:10,120
And then we begin again.

224
00:34:10,120 --> 00:34:16,120
Money becomes relatively cheaper and we
enter another high growth phase.

225
00:34:17,120 --> 00:34:30,120
But when we look at this cycle, obviously
through this mechanism, companies are very
important because they are the engines of
driving higher growth.

226
00:34:31,120 --> 00:34:33,120
Individuals are consumers.

227
00:34:33,120 --> 00:34:35,120
Companies are investors.

228
00:34:36,120 --> 00:34:37,120
Companies are driving growth.

229
00:34:38,120 --> 00:34:47,120
If we're going to understand how bond
markets really affect growth and company
earnings, let's understand what the
risk-free rate is.

230
00:34:48,120 --> 00:34:57,120
There are several proxies for the
risk-free rate, but here we are going to
use the 10-year U.S. Treasury note rate.

231
00:34:58,120 --> 00:35:03,120
It's the most widely tracked government
debt instrument in finance and it's the
main benchmark.

232
00:35:04,120 --> 00:35:21,120
As mentioned previously, the yield of the
10-year Treasury bond is often used as a
benchmark for the market to set other
interest rates, such as corporate bonds,
swaps, mortgages, et cetera, and fixed
rate loans.

233
00:35:22,120 --> 00:35:31,120
So, we are going to use the risk-free rate
and we're going to use the benchmark of
the 10-year U.S. Treasury note.

234
00:35:32,120 --> 00:35:35,120
That is going to be our risk-free rate
going forward.

235
00:35:36,120 --> 00:35:40,120
So, what does this mean for companies and
company earnings?

236
00:35:41,120 --> 00:35:52,120
Well, a very important formula used by
equity markets to value companies is the
discounted cash flow formula.

237
00:35:53,120 --> 00:36:04,120
And if you look at the formula here, the
DCF, the discounted cash flow, the present
value of all future cash flows is the DCF.

238
00:36:05,120 --> 00:36:16,120
And in the formula on the top, you can see
cash flow one, cash flow two, cash flow
all the way out to infinity in the future.

239
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:27,120
And you can see the discount factor of
those cash flows is one plus the risk-free
rate to the power of the period.

240
00:36:27,120 --> 00:36:28,120
Okay.

241
00:36:29,120 --> 00:36:30,120
Now, what happens here?

242
00:36:32,120 --> 00:36:45,120
Well, if we have a lower discount factor,
so the R, the risk-free rate, if we have a
lower discount factor, this gives us a
higher present value.

243
00:36:46,120 --> 00:36:49,120
We have a higher value for the DCF number.

244
00:36:49,120 --> 00:36:56,120
So, we're getting a higher present value
of all future cash flows.

245
00:36:57,120 --> 00:37:04,120
A higher discount factor gives us a lower
present value of all future cash flows.

246
00:37:05,120 --> 00:37:16,120
So, the risk-free rate, which is priced by
equity markets, it's used by equity
markets and priced off the benchmark.

247
00:37:16,120 --> 00:37:27,120
And here we're using the 10-year Treasury
note rate, is a crucial factor in equity
markets for valuing stocks and the stock
market itself.

248
00:37:28,120 --> 00:37:30,120
So, this is how it links together.

249
00:37:31,120 --> 00:37:32,120
Now, we brought it up earlier.

250
00:37:35,120 --> 00:37:36,120
Very, very important.

251
00:37:38,120 --> 00:37:39,120
Price-Earnings Ratio.

252
00:37:40,120 --> 00:37:44,120
It is the most widely used valuation
measure of an equity index.

253
00:37:45,120 --> 00:38:02,120
The price of the S&P 500, for example,
divided by the EPS of the S&P 500 is the
price-earnings Ratio of the S&P 500.

254
00:38:03,120 --> 00:38:12,120
So, we're looking at the level of the S&P
500 and dividing it by the earnings per
share of the S&P 500.

255
00:38:14,120 --> 00:38:37,120
When all things being equal, real interest
rates are close to or even less than zero,
the S&P 500 commands a higher PE valuation
multiple because future earnings
expectations are discounted back by the
market to a higher present value.

256
00:38:38,120 --> 00:38:50,120
Now, the risk-free rate is a subjective,
yet a fundamental driver of equity
valuation when we're using DCF analysis.

257
00:38:51,120 --> 00:39:03,120
As we go through that interest rate cycle
that we looked at earlier, equities get
repriced higher and lower according to
where real interest rates are trading.

258
00:39:03,120 --> 00:39:07,120
And we'll get on to the definition of real
interest rates in a second.

259
00:39:08,120 --> 00:39:23,120
We absolutely need to know, as traders,
the macroeconomic conditions for real
interest rates and central bank intentions
around setting interest rates and real
interest rates.

260
00:39:24,120 --> 00:39:50,120
Now, as you're probably aware, over the
last decade, central banks have been
intervening in the bond market and the
action is they've been buying bonds to
artificially keep both nominal and real
rates low in order to provide liquidity
and inflation to prolong the expansionary
part of the cycle.

261
00:39:51,120 --> 00:39:55,120
By expanding nominal and real GDP growth.

262
00:39:56,120 --> 00:40:04,120
This in itself also causes equity market
valuations to remain at elevated levels.

263
00:40:05,120 --> 00:40:12,120
Now, we're not going to be covering the
central bank actions themselves in the PTM
video series because these will change
over time.

264
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:17,120
What's important to understand here is the
implications.

265
00:40:17,120 --> 00:40:22,120
This is what's going to stand the test of
time in your overall trading framework.

266
00:40:23,120 --> 00:40:29,120
So you can apply this template at any
moment in time to any macroeconomic
conditions.

267
00:40:30,120 --> 00:40:36,120
So you're probably wondering what is the
difference between nominal and real
interest rates.

268
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:43,120
Well, distinguishing between the two is
very important.

269
00:40:44,120 --> 00:40:50,120
And it's at the core of any interest rate
decision or central bank agenda.

270
00:40:51,120 --> 00:40:58,120
The real interest rate is simply the
nominal interest rate minus the level of
inflation.

271
00:40:59,120 --> 00:41:16,120
In simple terms, real interest rates mean
everything for asset allocation in the
financial markets and the investment
decisions of entities like pension funds,
mutual funds, hedge funds, everyone on the
professional side.

272
00:41:17,120 --> 00:41:21,120
And nominal interest rates themselves mean
very little.

273
00:41:22,120 --> 00:41:23,120
Why is this?

274
00:41:23,120 --> 00:41:27,120
Well, let's say the official rate is 5%
again.

275
00:41:28,120 --> 00:41:35,120
What does a 5% official interest rate
return in a period of 5% inflation?

276
00:41:36,120 --> 00:41:37,120
Well, in real terms, zero.

277
00:41:37,120 --> 00:41:47,120
So in the United States, we adjust for
CPI, the consumer price index, which is
the level of inflation.

278
00:41:48,120 --> 00:42:01,120
We adjust for CPI and in other countries
around the world, all you have to do is
adjust the official rate by the official
rate of inflation in that particular
country.

279
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:11,120
Now, what we're going to look at to get a
sense of how important real interest rates
actually are.

280
00:42:12,120 --> 00:42:22,120
Let's have a look at this visually and
have a look at real interest rates
compared to the level of the stock market
and stock market valuation.

281
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:34,120
So if you look at this chart on the screen
here, you'll see in the bottom left-hand
corner, you'll see a key and there's three
variables here.

282
00:42:35,120 --> 00:42:38,120
There's three lines on the chart.

283
00:42:39,120 --> 00:42:42,120
Have a look at the key in the bottom
left-hand corner.

284
00:42:42,120 --> 00:42:51,120
At the very bottom and denoted in pink on
the chart, we have the last price of the
real 10-year yield.

285
00:42:52,120 --> 00:42:57,120
So the nominal level of interest rates
minus inflation.

286
00:42:57,120 --> 00:42:59,120
So the official rate minus CPI.

287
00:43:00,120 --> 00:43:09,120
And if we just cast our eye onto the
chart, if you just have a look at the pink
line and just look in the middle of the
chart.

288
00:43:09,120 --> 00:43:22,120
So around 2010, 2011, you'll see that the
real interest rate in the US was around
two to two and a half around this level.

289
00:43:22,120 --> 00:43:35,120
So the nominal rates adjusted for
inflation, the real interest rate in the
US, 2010, 2011, denoted by that pink line
around two to two and a half.

290
00:43:36,120 --> 00:43:38,120
Then we just see a big move down.

291
00:43:39,120 --> 00:43:40,120
And it goes negative.

292
00:43:41,120 --> 00:43:43,120
It goes below the zero band.

293
00:43:44,120 --> 00:43:46,120
So we have negative real interest rates.

294
00:43:47,120 --> 00:44:03,120
And you can see the effect that that
started to have on the white line and the
green line, which if you look back to the
key in the bottom left-hand corner, is the
price of the stock market, the S&P 500
index.

295
00:44:04,120 --> 00:44:08,120
And the green line, which is the actual
valuation of stocks.

296
00:44:08,120 --> 00:44:11,120
So the price earnings ratio of the S&P
500.

297
00:44:12,120 --> 00:44:25,120
And if you look back at the pink line, the
pink line did a bit of work, kind of
moving sideways between slightly below the
zero band up to 1% on a few occasions in
the last decade.

298
00:44:26,120 --> 00:44:29,120
And then in 2020, boom, it goes negative.

299
00:44:30,120 --> 00:44:30,120
Okay.

300
00:44:31,120 --> 00:44:40,120
So, how does that affect the stock market
and how does that affect the price
earnings ratio of the stock market?

301
00:44:41,120 --> 00:44:50,120
Well, you can see on the chart in the last
couple of years, you can see that the
stock market and valuations have gone
through the roof.

302
00:44:51,120 --> 00:45:04,120
And that is essentially Fed policy being
accommodative to expansion and maintaining
expansion and high stock market levels and
high stock market valuations.

303
00:45:05,120 --> 00:45:11,120
So, you can see the green line, where the
green line and the white line kind of come
together on the chart.

304
00:45:12,120 --> 00:45:18,120
You'll see that that level for the P ratio
for the S&P 500 is around 30.

305
00:45:19,120 --> 00:45:23,120
And then the dashed line is the forward P
estimate.

306
00:45:24,120 --> 00:45:29,120
So, that's coming out in 2021 at 22.85.

307
00:45:30,120 --> 00:45:32,120
But that's just a forecast.

308
00:45:32,120 --> 00:45:40,120
And if you look at the reported data of
2020, we see the price earnings ratio of
the S&P 500.

309
00:45:41,120 --> 00:45:52,120
When the S&P was around 3700, we see the P
ratio of around 30, which is obviously
extremely elevated in terms of valuation.

310
00:45:53,120 --> 00:45:56,120
But let's have a look at this in slightly
closer detail.

311
00:45:56,120 --> 00:46:08,120
If we look at US 10-year real interest
rates, so adjusted, nominal rates adjusted
for CPI, we've charted this against the
VIX, which we'll come on to later.

312
00:46:09,120 --> 00:46:21,120
But you can see if you look at interest
rates in 2019, real interest rates in
2019, and you're looking at the right-hand
axis here on the chart.

313
00:46:22,120 --> 00:46:26,120
So, the right-hand axis denotes the real
10-year yield.

314
00:46:27,120 --> 00:46:31,120
So, the nominal yield adjusted for CPI in
the US.

315
00:46:32,120 --> 00:46:41,120
You can see in 2019, we just see the real
interest rate in the US collapsing, and in
2020, going negative.

316
00:46:42,120 --> 00:46:51,120
So, you can see how the real interest
rate, how important it is, how it affects
stock market level and stock market
valuation.

317
00:46:52,120 --> 00:47:03,120
But one of the macroeconomic implications
here, well, real interest rates, whether
it's market rates, official rates, they
represent the real price or cost of money.

318
00:47:04,120 --> 00:47:09,120
All things being equal, if money is
cheaper, market participants will borrow
more of it.

319
00:47:10,120 --> 00:47:13,120
And when it's expensive, they will borrow
less of it.

320
00:47:14,120 --> 00:47:15,120
So, let's get this down.

321
00:47:16,120 --> 00:47:27,120
Relatively cheaper money causes more
borrowing, more liquidity, and
inflationary conditions and expansionary
GDP.

322
00:47:29,120 --> 00:47:39,120
Relatively expensive money creates less
borrowing, less liquidity, and
deflationary conditions, and
contractionary GDP.

323
00:47:40,120 --> 00:47:43,120
And listen, central banks know this,
they're not stupid, okay?

324
00:47:44,120 --> 00:47:47,120
Central banks are looking at the same
things that we are looking at.

325
00:47:48,120 --> 00:48:10,120
They're continually looking to manipulate
the supply of funds, which is real
interest rates, via the interest rate
mechanism, setting official interest
rates, or even doing abnormal policy, such
as intervening in the bond markets to
affect market rates.

326
00:48:11,120 --> 00:48:32,120
So, incidentally, and I said we would come
on to this a little bit later, in terms of
the VIX and looking at real interest rates
compared to the VIX, you'll find that
relatively cheaper money, so lower real
rates, is going to create less equity
volatility because liquidity is higher.

327
00:48:33,120 --> 00:48:41,120
And relatively expensive money creates
more equity volatility, because there's
less liquidity.

328
00:48:42,120 --> 00:48:54,120
So, putting all this together, it stands
to reason that we can use real interest
rates in the US as a leading indicator to
equity market forecasts.

329
00:48:55,120 --> 00:49:05,120
So, the level of the market and the
valuation of the market, and also the
direction and the volatility of the stock
market.

330
00:49:06,120 --> 00:49:15,120
Now, what we're going to look at moving on
is the relationship between short-term and
long-term money.

331
00:49:16,120 --> 00:49:20,120
So, the price of money in the short-term
relative to the long-term, okay?

332
00:49:21,120 --> 00:49:25,120
So, this is basically yield curve
analysis.

333
00:49:26,120 --> 00:49:28,120
So, we're looking at something called the
yield curve.

334
00:49:29,120 --> 00:49:36,120
Now, what you're going to find in the
downloads area of this video, you're going
to find a couple of spreadsheets, which
are pretty big.

335
00:49:37,120 --> 00:49:41,120
But you should definitely download them,
open them up, and start playing with them.

336
00:49:42,120 --> 00:49:48,120
You'll see we've got one called benchmark
yields US and benchmark yields rest of the
world.

337
00:49:49,120 --> 00:49:52,120
And you're going to find all of this
analysis that we're going to go through in
those spreadsheets.

338
00:49:53,120 --> 00:49:57,120
But first, let's talk about some basic
bond relationships.

339
00:49:58,120 --> 00:50:04,120
Firstly, and this should have started to
become quite obvious to you at this stage.

340
00:50:05,120 --> 00:50:10,120
The price of a bond is inversely
correlated to its yield.

341
00:50:11,120 --> 00:50:21,120
So, if demand for bonds increases, so
there's more buyers than sellers, the
bonds price will rise and the yield of
that bond will fall.

342
00:50:22,120 --> 00:50:23,120
And the reverse is true.

343
00:50:24,120 --> 00:50:25,120
Now, what does a yield curve do?

344
00:50:25,120 --> 00:50:43,120
Well, a yield curve plots the interest
rates of bonds of an issuer that have
equal credit at different maturities.

345
00:50:44,120 --> 00:50:54,120
The yield curve of the US government's
bonds plots the yields of differing US
Treasury bond maturities that have been
issued by the US government.

346
00:50:55,120 --> 00:50:56,120
And we know what it's used for.

347
00:50:56,120 --> 00:50:59,120
The money is borrowed and used to finance
public spending.

348
00:51:00,120 --> 00:51:05,120
But we can actually plot the yield curve
and look at this visually.

349
00:51:06,120 --> 00:51:17,120
So, we look at the US government Treasury
yields at different maturities and we
literally plot the yield curve.

350
00:51:18,120 --> 00:51:25,120
So, here, and you'll notice this analysis
in the spreadsheet that you download in
the downloads area.

351
00:51:26,120 --> 00:51:31,120
Here, we're plotting two yield curves at
two different moments in time.

352
00:51:32,120 --> 00:51:41,120
So, first of all, we're looking at, most
recently, what the yield curve looked at
on the 3rd of January 2021.

353
00:51:42,120 --> 00:51:44,120
And this is the blue line, okay?

354
00:51:45,120 --> 00:51:49,120
And we can see across the bottom, we've
got the Fed funds rate, the official rate.

355
00:51:50,120 --> 00:51:53,120
And we have different maturities across
the curve.

356
00:51:54,120 --> 00:51:59,120
So, we have short term, so we have one
month, three month, six month, one year,
two year.

357
00:52:00,120 --> 00:52:04,120
And then we have, all the way to the right
hand side, longer term rates.

358
00:52:04,120 --> 00:52:06,120
We have the 10 year and the 20 year.

359
00:52:06,120 --> 00:52:08,120
And then we have everything else in
between.

360
00:52:08,120 --> 00:52:11,120
So, medium term rates, three year, five
year, seven year.

361
00:52:12,120 --> 00:52:19,120
And we can see from the blue line, if we
take a snapshot of what the yield curve
looked like on the 3rd of January 2021,

362
00:52:20,120 --> 00:52:23,120
we have an upward sloping curve.

363
00:52:24,120 --> 00:52:30,120
We also took the data from the 8th of
November 2015.

364
00:52:31,120 --> 00:52:35,120
And we plot that yield curve as well
across the maturities.

365
00:52:36,120 --> 00:52:43,120
And we can see on the 8th of November
2015, we had an upward sloping yield
curve.

366
00:52:44,120 --> 00:52:47,120
So, how does this actually work and what
does it all mean?

367
00:52:47,120 --> 00:52:57,120
Well, the chart that you've just seen,
plots the yields on differing US
Government Treasury bonds from two
different dates.

368
00:52:58,120 --> 00:53:02,120
The yield curve is dynamic and it changes
every day, okay?

369
00:53:03,120 --> 00:53:06,120
So, that yield curve will look different
next week.

370
00:53:07,120 --> 00:53:09,120
It will look different in a month's time.

371
00:53:10,120 --> 00:53:12,120
The rates are changing dynamically, okay?

372
00:53:13,120 --> 00:53:27,120
If we take a snapshot at any one point in
time, the demand and supply of bonds, so
therefore yields, by the market changes
because of expectations of both short term
and longer term rates.

373
00:53:28,120 --> 00:53:35,120
And those expectations are based on the
market's economic projections of growth
and central bank policy.

374
00:53:36,120 --> 00:53:39,120
Now, there's three different types of
yield curve.

375
00:53:40,120 --> 00:53:45,120
Three different shapes of the yield curve
that represent conditions, okay?

376
00:53:46,120 --> 00:53:49,120
Firstly, we have a normal yield curve.

377
00:53:50,120 --> 00:53:52,120
Second, a flat yield curve.

378
00:53:53,120 --> 00:53:55,120
And third, we have an inverted yield
curve.

379
00:53:56,120 --> 00:54:04,120
So, we're going to go through these and
show you what it all means and how you can
use the yield curve as a leading
indicator.

380
00:54:06,120 --> 00:54:09,120
So, what is a normal yield curve?

381
00:54:10,120 --> 00:54:12,120
Well, it's what you just saw on the
screen.

382
00:54:13,120 --> 00:54:14,120
It's an upward sloping yield curve.

383
00:54:15,120 --> 00:54:19,120
It's where longer term rates are higher
than shorter term rates.

384
00:54:20,120 --> 00:54:30,120
And most of the time, and this is why we
call it a normal yield curve, most of the
time, what you're going to find is the
yield curve is upward sloping.

385
00:54:31,120 --> 00:54:36,120
That's because in normal economic
conditions, we have economic expansion.

386
00:54:37,120 --> 00:54:38,120
This occurs most of the time.

387
00:54:39,120 --> 00:54:52,120
During that time, the market demands a
higher interest rate, so yield, when
lending money to an issuer longer term due
to the risk of time, okay?

388
00:54:53,120 --> 00:55:00,120
In a normal market, the longer time an
investor's money is tied up, the higher
the yield they're going to demand.

389
00:55:01,120 --> 00:55:09,120
And most importantly, an upward sloping
yield curve signifies an expectation by
the market of future economic expansion.

390
00:55:11,120 --> 00:55:14,120
So, on a relative basis, what does this
actually suggest?

391
00:55:15,120 --> 00:55:27,120
Well, a normal yield curve, so an upward
sloping curve, suggests that investors, on
a relative basis, are selling longer term
bonds.

392
00:55:28,120 --> 00:55:32,120
So, there's higher rates, okay, to buy
shorter term bonds.

393
00:55:33,120 --> 00:55:37,120
And because of the buying, there's lower
rates, okay?

394
00:55:38,120 --> 00:55:41,120
So, there's two main reasons for this.

395
00:55:42,120 --> 00:55:44,120
So, firstly, buying short term bonds.

396
00:55:45,120 --> 00:55:55,120
Well, if investors are expecting an
economic expansion, they don't want their
money tied up, long term, in low yielding
assets.

397
00:55:56,120 --> 00:56:00,120
What do they want to be doing if they're
expecting economic expansion?

398
00:56:00,120 --> 00:56:09,120
They want to buy risk assets, like
equities, and they have a preference for
shorter term liquidity.

399
00:56:10,120 --> 00:56:16,120
And what's happening on the other side of
the curve, in long term bonds, on a
relative basis?

400
00:56:17,120 --> 00:56:29,120
Well, investors, because they have a
preference for shorter term liquidity, in
an expected expansionary phase, they are
selling long term bonds on a relative
basis, okay?

401
00:56:30,120 --> 00:56:39,120
They can park their money in shorter term
bonds, and later, they can buy back their
longer term bonds at a lower price.

402
00:56:40,120 --> 00:56:41,120
So, a higher bond yield.

403
00:56:41,120 --> 00:56:42,120
So, a higher bond yield.

404
00:56:42,120 --> 00:56:51,120
Later, in the future, they can do that
when they expect an economic expansion is
coming to an end.

405
00:56:52,120 --> 00:56:59,120
So, they would actually take the opposite
action when they expect that the expansion
is ending.

406
00:56:59,120 --> 00:57:07,120
They would sell short term bonds and buy
long term bonds at a lower price and
higher bond yield.

407
00:57:08,120 --> 00:57:09,120
That's what they're doing with their
money.

408
00:57:09,120 --> 00:57:32,120
They're shifting their money out of long
term bonds and into short term bonds, so
they can have a shorter term liquidity and
a preference for that to enable them to
take advantage of the expansion and to
park their money in risk assets, like in
the stock market.

409
00:57:32,120 --> 00:57:46,120
And then later, when they expect the
economic expansion to come to an end, they
are forecasting that bond prices will be
lower and yields will be higher, and then
they take the opposite action.

410
00:57:47,120 --> 00:57:53,120
They sell short term bonds and buy long
term bonds and get a higher yield on the
long term bonds.

411
00:57:54,120 --> 00:58:02,120
So, to break this down, when investors
expect economic expansion, what happens to
the yield curve?

412
00:58:03,120 --> 00:58:05,120
The yield curve steepens.

413
00:58:06,120 --> 00:58:12,120
When they expect economic contraction, the
yield curve flattens or inverts.

414
00:58:13,120 --> 00:58:17,120
So, when investors are expecting economic
expansion, what is happening?

415
00:58:18,120 --> 00:58:21,120
They're selling long term bonds to buy
short term bonds.

416
00:58:22,120 --> 00:58:23,120
What does that mean?

417
00:58:24,120 --> 00:58:30,120
Well, we're getting higher yields on
longer term bonds and lower yields in the
market on short term bonds.

418
00:58:31,120 --> 00:58:35,120
So, the curve, the yield curve is
steepening, okay?

419
00:58:36,120 --> 00:58:41,120
And then what happens in the opposite
situation when economic contraction is
occurring?

420
00:58:42,120 --> 00:58:45,120
Well, we're now doing the opposite, okay?

421
00:58:45,120 --> 00:58:51,120
The market is selling short term bonds to
buy long term bonds.

422
00:58:52,120 --> 00:58:59,120
They're putting their money into longer
term bonds because they expect a
contraction is going to happen.

423
00:59:01,120 --> 00:59:11,120
And the more long term bonds that the
market buys and the more short term bonds
that they sell, what's going to happen?

424
00:59:12,120 --> 00:59:19,120
Well, long term yields are going to go up
and short term yields are going to go
down.

425
00:59:20,120 --> 00:59:26,120
And on a relative basis, this means that
the yield curve is going to flatten and
potentially invert.

426
00:59:27,120 --> 00:59:32,120
Inversion is when short term rates are
higher than long term rates.

427
00:59:33,120 --> 00:59:41,120
So, a normal yield curve happens most of
the time because economic expansion is
occurring.

428
00:59:42,120 --> 00:59:50,120
And economic expansion, that is the
condition that is occurring most of the
time in normal economic conditions.

429
00:59:51,120 --> 01:00:03,120
And of course, occasionally, we have
economic contraction in the economy.

430
01:00:04,120 --> 01:00:16,120
And that creates an abnormal situation in
the yield curve where we have a flat yield
curve occurring and also sometimes
inversion.

431
01:00:19,120 --> 01:00:22,120
Now, we saw a normal yield curve earlier.

432
01:00:22,120 --> 01:00:30,120
What we've done here, we've put on the
chart, utilising the spreadsheets from the
downloads area.

433
01:00:31,120 --> 01:00:37,120
We've put on the chart two normal looking
yield curve from two moments in history.

434
01:00:38,120 --> 01:00:43,120
We've got the blue line from December
2020, which looks fairly normal.

435
01:00:43,120 --> 01:00:53,120
And you can see that rates overall are
lower compared to January 2003, where we
plot the yield curve at that moment in
time.

436
01:00:54,120 --> 01:00:56,120
And we have a normal yield curve there as
well.

437
01:00:57,120 --> 01:01:03,120
So, what we're looking at here is two
moments in time where there was a normal
yield curve.

438
01:01:04,120 --> 01:01:11,120
And you can see at the bottom of the
screen two sliders that are in the
spreadsheet in the downloads area.

439
01:01:12,120 --> 01:01:13,120
And you can play with these sliders.

440
01:01:14,120 --> 01:01:17,120
You can plot yield curves from different
moments in time.

441
01:01:18,120 --> 01:01:34,120
And even at similar moments in time, you
can move the slider using the arrows and
compare two curves when they, for example,
go from normal to flat to inverting and
then back again.

442
01:01:34,120 --> 01:01:37,120
So, inverting flat to normal.

443
01:01:37,120 --> 01:01:47,120
And that's typically around when economic
expansions become contractions and
contractions turn into expansions.

444
01:01:47,120 --> 01:01:49,120
It's a very useful thing to do.

445
01:01:49,120 --> 01:01:54,120
Just go back in time and have a look at
different moments in time and plot the
yield curves.

446
01:01:55,120 --> 01:01:58,120
It's actually fun and it's a good lesson
in economic history.

447
01:01:59,120 --> 01:02:03,120
And, of course, a good lesson in financial
markets history.

448
01:02:04,120 --> 01:02:15,120
So, let's have a look at a nice lesson in
financial markets history here where we
have a look at a yield curve that's
actually in the process of inverting.

449
01:02:16,120 --> 01:02:23,120
So, we've got two lines on the chart that
we've taken from two moments in time where
we're plotting the yield curve.

450
01:02:24,120 --> 01:02:31,120
And the two moments in time that we're
plotting is November 2019 and February
2020.

451
01:02:31,120 --> 01:02:49,120
So, this is essentially going into the
COVID era when the markets got hit in
February 2020 and got smashed in March
2020 very, very aggressively where the
stock market went down a lot.

452
01:02:49,120 --> 01:02:50,120
Okay.

453
01:02:50,120 --> 01:02:56,120
So, if we look on the bottom of the chart,
we can see the Fed Fund's official rates.

454
01:02:57,120 --> 01:03:02,120
And we can see the maturities from one
month out to 20 years.

455
01:03:03,120 --> 01:03:08,120
And what we're interested in here is
looking at inversion occurring.

456
01:03:08,120 --> 01:03:09,120
Okay.

457
01:03:09,120 --> 01:03:27,120
So, if we look at the yield curve on the
right-hand side, you will see the rates
plotted in a table where the Fed Fund's
rate in November 2019 was 1.55.

458
01:03:27,120 --> 01:03:36,120
And you can see looking down through the
table from one month to 20 years that the
numbers just get higher and higher.

459
01:03:36,120 --> 01:03:37,120
Okay.

460
01:03:38,120 --> 01:03:42,120
That is the characteristic of a normal
yield curve.

461
01:03:42,120 --> 01:03:45,120
And that's plotted on the chart in orange.

462
01:03:46,120 --> 01:03:55,120
Now, let's have a look in the table on the
right-hand side at February 2, 2020, where
the Fed Fund's rate was 1.59.

463
01:03:56,120 --> 01:04:03,120
If we look at the 10-year at that moment
in time, we can see the 10-year yield is
1.51.

464
01:04:04,120 --> 01:04:13,120
And if we look at short-term rates, so we
look at one month, it's 1.56, which is
obviously higher than 1.51.

465
01:04:14,120 --> 01:04:16,120
Three months, 1.55.

466
01:04:16,120 --> 01:04:18,120
Six months, 1.54.

467
01:04:18,120 --> 01:04:21,120
And then the one year is 1.45.

468
01:04:21,120 --> 01:04:22,120
Okay.

469
01:04:22,120 --> 01:04:35,120
So what we see here is in the short end of
the market, 1-month, 3-month and 6-month
money, the yield is trading higher than
the 10-year.

470
01:04:36,120 --> 01:04:47,120
So what's interesting is in terms of the
price action, is that if we compare the
short-term rates of 1-month, 3-month,
6-month,

471
01:04:47,120 --> 01:04:56,120
on February 2, 2020 to November 10, 2019,
we can see that they're fairly similar.

472
01:04:56,120 --> 01:05:06,120
But when we compare the 10-year, which was
1.94 on November 10, 2019, by Feb 2, 2020,
it was 1.51.

473
01:05:07,120 --> 01:05:24,120
So what is being characterised in this
market is very obviously buying of
long-term bonds, which is suppressing the
yield and the yield is going down on the
10-year.

474
01:05:25,120 --> 01:05:37,120
So on a relative basis, longer-term yields
have gone lower because of buying in the
market of long-term bonds.

475
01:05:37,120 --> 01:05:39,120
So what's the market telling you here?

476
01:05:39,120 --> 01:05:53,120
Well, the market is telling you that
between the period November 10, 2019 and
February 2, 2020, that there was a
preference in the market to buy long-term
bonds.

477
01:05:53,120 --> 01:06:01,120
And during that period from November 10,
2019 to February 2, 2020, what would have
been happening?

478
01:06:02,120 --> 01:06:11,120
Well, the yield curve will have been
flattening and then in the process of
inverting.

479
01:06:12,120 --> 01:06:27,120
So the market was sending us a very strong
signal before COVID hit and had its
biggest impact at the end of February 2020
and March 2020 and even in April.

480
01:06:30,120 --> 01:06:42,120
So the market was sending us the market
was sending us the message that they were
expecting economic contraction and the
yield curve was in the process of
inverting before it happened.

481
01:06:43,120 --> 01:07:01,120
Now, in terms of market benchmarks and
reliability of using the yield curve as an
indicator or even better as a leading
indicator to economic expansion and
contraction.

482
01:07:02,120 --> 01:07:18,120
The market, in terms of benchmarks,
gravitates to and uses the tens minus two
spread, meaning if we look at the 10-year
and the two-year price of money.

483
01:07:19,120 --> 01:07:40,120
So in the chart, for example, we're
looking at 151 and 133 when we take the
snapshot on February 2, 2020, the market
uses this, the tens minus twos as an
anchor, as a benchmark, as an indicator
for economic contraction.

484
01:07:40,120 --> 01:07:48,120
So the potential for recession, two
negative quarters of GDP growth.

485
01:07:49,120 --> 01:07:58,120
And it's interesting that at this moment
in time, we can see that the yield curve
is in the process of inverting.

486
01:08:00,120 --> 01:08:08,120
But it's inverting on February 2, 2020,
only in the very short end of the curve.

487
01:08:09,120 --> 01:08:25,120
As an exercise, what you can do is go into
the spreadsheet in the downloads area and
play with the slider and take yourself
back to February 2, 2020, and then move
forward week by week.

488
01:08:26,120 --> 01:08:35,120
And you can see whether or not the tens
minus twos spread actually went negative.

489
01:08:36,120 --> 01:08:37,120
What do we mean by that?

490
01:08:38,120 --> 01:08:44,120
Well, meaning that the 10-year minus the
two-year was less than zero.

491
01:08:45,120 --> 01:08:49,120
That being the two-year was higher than
the 10-year yield.

492
01:08:50,120 --> 01:08:52,120
So you can do that in your own time.

493
01:08:53,120 --> 01:09:07,120
And also, of course, look at all economic
expansions and contractions in the past
and have a look at what the yield curve
actually did and have a look at what the
tens minus twos actually did.

494
01:09:07,120 --> 01:09:24,120
We can actually chart the tens minus twos
and have a look at moments in history and
learn some important information about
macroeconomics in the past and financial
markets history.

495
01:09:25,120 --> 01:09:35,120
So if we look back here in 2019, for
example, so we go back a little bit
further than we were looking at on the
chart on the previous slide.

496
01:09:36,120 --> 01:09:43,120
We can see that in 2019, we can see that
in 2019, earlier in the year, what was
happening?

497
01:09:43,120 --> 01:09:55,120
Well, you can see in the summer of 2019,
the yield curve, the tens minus twos began
to invert.

498
01:09:55,120 --> 01:10:08,120
And it actually went to the zero bound,
the tens minus twos went to the zero
bound, which means the yield on the
two-year was the same as the 10-year.

499
01:10:08,120 --> 01:10:11,120
And then later in the year, it actually
steepened.

500
01:10:11,120 --> 01:10:18,120
The curve actually steepened where
relatively 10-year rates were higher than
two-year rates.

501
01:10:18,120 --> 01:10:24,120
But what's interesting about this is that
it never went negative.

502
01:10:24,120 --> 01:10:27,120
That's an important moment in financial
markets history.

503
01:10:27,120 --> 01:10:29,120
And we'll come on to that a little bit
later.

504
01:10:29,120 --> 01:10:43,120
But what the bond market was telling us
here at this moment in time, summer 2019,
was that the US economy was expected to
slow down and possibly contract and be in
trouble.

505
01:10:43,120 --> 01:10:52,120
Now, we can even compare this moment in
time, going way, way back to previous
contractions.

506
01:10:53,120 --> 01:11:09,120
So on the right-hand side here, you can
see, we've got the 2019 period, where
we've highlighted the moment in summer
2019, where the tens minus twos went to
the zero bound.

507
01:11:09,120 --> 01:11:13,120
So 10-year and two-year yields were
basically the same.

508
01:11:13,120 --> 01:11:23,120
And we've highlighted going way back to
the 2006-07 period that preceded the
global financial crisis.

509
01:11:23,120 --> 01:11:36,120
You can see in the 2006-2007 period, in
2006, the tens minus twos went below the
zero bound, meaning two-year yields were
higher than 10-year yields.

510
01:11:36,120 --> 01:11:43,120
And of course, they went positive, we can
see that visually on the chart in 2006 at
a period of time.

511
01:11:43,120 --> 01:11:54,120
But for most of 2006, and a good portion
of 2007, the tens minus twos was actually
negative.

512
01:11:54,120 --> 01:12:05,120
The spread was negative, below the zero
bound, indicating that the market is
expecting significant contraction in the
US economy.

513
01:12:05,120 --> 01:12:07,120
And then, of course, what did we get?

514
01:12:07,120 --> 01:12:17,120
Following that, in the second half of 2007
and all of 2008, we had economic recession
and financial markets collapse.

515
01:12:17,120 --> 01:12:20,120
We had a liquidity crisis.

516
01:12:20,120 --> 01:12:23,120
So what did we get in 2019?

517
01:12:23,120 --> 01:12:26,120
Well, the Fed's not stupid.

518
01:12:26,120 --> 01:12:32,120
They were looking at the same things that
everyone else was looking at in the
markets, what we were looking at.

519
01:12:32,120 --> 01:12:45,120
They could see what the market was
expecting in terms of potential economic
contraction because of their preference
for long-term bonds over short-term bonds.

520
01:12:45,120 --> 01:12:48,120
They could see what was happening in the
US economy.

521
01:12:48,120 --> 01:12:53,120
They could see a slowdown occurring and
they decided to take action.

522
01:12:53,120 --> 01:13:10,120
In the summer of 2019, they were active in
the market in lowering nominal rates,
increasing liquidity in the bond market
and therefore increasing liquidity in risk
assets and lowering volatility in the
equities market.

523
01:13:10,120 --> 01:13:22,120
So a good six months, seven months before
COVID, the US economy was arguably in
trouble.

524
01:13:22,120 --> 01:13:29,120
The Fed's actions in the summer of 2019
arguably saved the market.

525
01:13:29,120 --> 01:13:40,120
And obviously, the market in the bond
market, the yield curve started to
steepen.

526
01:13:40,120 --> 01:13:51,120
And we can see that on the chart in late
2019 when the yield curve started to
steepen and go more positive.

527
01:13:51,120 --> 01:14:05,120
And arguably, it was COVID that actually
sent the US economy, the exogenous shock
of COVID sent the US economy into a
tailspin.

528
01:14:05,120 --> 01:14:11,120
By no means am I saying here that the US
economy was, open quotes, fixed, close
quotes.

529
01:14:11,120 --> 01:14:20,120
But a potential crisis that was occurring
in the summer of 2019 was averted by the
Fed actions.

530
01:14:21,120 --> 01:14:23,120
And it was actually expected.

531
01:14:23,120 --> 01:14:26,120
It was an expected move by the Fed.

532
01:14:26,120 --> 01:14:29,120
We'll get onto this a little bit later.

533
01:14:29,120 --> 01:14:35,120
But you've got to remember, the Fed is
looking at the same things that we look
at.

534
01:14:35,120 --> 01:14:36,120
They're not stupid.

535
01:14:36,120 --> 01:14:38,120
They're looking at the tens minus twos.

536
01:14:38,120 --> 01:14:40,120
They're looking at the yield curve.

537
01:14:40,120 --> 01:14:48,120
They're looking at the relationship
between short and long term money and what
signals the market is sending.

538
01:14:48,120 --> 01:14:52,120
And the Fed tends to act accordingly.

539
01:14:52,120 --> 01:14:57,120
But we'll come onto this later in terms of
Fed policy.

540
01:14:57,120 --> 01:15:19,120
But it's just interesting to look at these
things and look at moments in financial
markets history to not only understand the
signals that the bond market sends as a
potential leading indicator to our
expectations of economic expansion and
contraction.

541
01:15:19,120 --> 01:15:39,120
It's important to understand economic
history and financial markets history just
to have a foundational awareness for our
macroeconomic learning from macroeconomic
understanding of the world.

542
01:15:39,120 --> 01:15:51,120
So why don't we have a look, for example,
at this moment in history during the 2005
to 2007 period.

543
01:15:51,120 --> 01:15:58,120
We know that in 2006, 2007, that the tens
minus twos was negative below the zero
bound.

544
01:15:58,120 --> 01:16:03,120
But what did things look like in 2005
compared to 2006?

545
01:16:03,120 --> 01:16:09,120
Well, we've charted this using the
spreadsheet tool that you have in the
downloads area.

546
01:16:09,120 --> 01:16:19,120
If we look at the orange line, we can see
we've taken a snapshot of the yield curve
on the 24th of July, 2005.

547
01:16:19,120 --> 01:16:23,120
At the time, the Fed funds rate was 325.

548
01:16:23,120 --> 01:16:31,120
And you can see across the curve, across
the maturities, we've got a pretty normal
looking yield curve.

549
01:16:31,120 --> 01:16:32,120
Okay.

550
01:16:32,120 --> 01:16:40,120
Fast forward to December 2006, 24th of
December 2006, the blue line.

551
01:16:40,120 --> 01:16:41,120
What have we got here?

552
01:16:41,120 --> 01:16:47,120
Well, Fed funds rate is now higher at 524.

553
01:16:47,120 --> 01:16:51,120
And we've now got an inverted yield curve.

554
01:16:51,120 --> 01:16:58,120
So if we look across the maturities, we've
got the 10 year at 463.

555
01:16:58,120 --> 01:17:02,120
What maturities are higher than 463?

556
01:17:02,120 --> 01:17:11,120
Well, we can look from one month, three
month, six month, to one year, two year,
and three year.

557
01:17:11,120 --> 01:17:16,120
So we can see that the tens minus twos
inverts.

558
01:17:16,120 --> 01:17:26,120
And basically the yield curve from one
month up to three years was inverted
relative to the 10 year.

559
01:17:26,120 --> 01:17:30,120
So it's interesting to look at these
things throughout financial markets
history.

560
01:17:30,120 --> 01:17:41,120
And I'd encourage you to keep doing it and
keep using that spreadsheet tool to really
get a foundational understanding of
macroeconomic history and financial
markets history.

561
01:17:41,120 --> 01:17:42,120
It really helps.

562
01:17:42,120 --> 01:17:51,120
So in terms of inverting, inverted yield
curves, what is it?

563
01:17:51,120 --> 01:17:53,120
Let's get this down.

564
01:17:53,120 --> 01:17:55,120
In inverted yield curves.

565
01:17:55,120 --> 01:18:03,120
It's when the yield on bonds with shorter
maturity are higher than yields on bonds
that have a longer maturity.

566
01:18:03,120 --> 01:18:09,120
It's an abnormal situation that occurs in
the minority of times.

567
01:18:09,120 --> 01:18:22,120
This situation signals from the bond
market expected financial stress and an
impending slowdown in GDP growth with
possible GDP contraction.

568
01:18:22,120 --> 01:18:25,120
So negative real GDP.

569
01:18:25,120 --> 01:18:36,120
And it could indicate possible recession,
which we know is defined as two
consecutive quarters of negative GDP
growth.

570
01:18:36,120 --> 01:18:40,120
So what is the market actually doing here?

571
01:18:40,120 --> 01:18:45,120
Well, they're selling short term bonds and
buying long term bonds.

572
01:18:45,120 --> 01:18:47,120
Let's start with selling short term bonds.

573
01:18:47,120 --> 01:18:48,120
What is happening?

574
01:18:48,120 --> 01:19:03,120
Well, the selling of short term bonds
creates higher yields in the short term,
in the short end of the curve relative to
the longer term, where buying of long term
bonds is occurring.

575
01:19:03,120 --> 01:19:07,120
So we're selling short term bonds.

576
01:19:07,120 --> 01:19:09,120
We get higher yields.

577
01:19:09,120 --> 01:19:17,120
When a yield curve inverts, it's because
investors have lost confidence in the
economy in the short term.

578
01:19:17,120 --> 01:19:24,120
They are demanding more yield for a short
term investment than for a long term one.

579
01:19:24,120 --> 01:19:26,120
And what are they doing with risk assets?

580
01:19:26,120 --> 01:19:38,120
They're selling risk assets like equities
as well, raising the money and they're
using that money to own long term bonds at
lower yields.

581
01:19:38,120 --> 01:19:41,120
So they are selling short term bonds.

582
01:19:41,120 --> 01:19:53,120
And they're selling risk assets and
raising money and putting that money and
parking it in long term bonds where
they're getting lower yields.

583
01:19:53,120 --> 01:20:04,120
They are happy to tie up their money for
years even though they're receiving lower
yields versus short term bonds.

584
01:20:04,120 --> 01:20:05,120
Why would they do this?

585
01:20:05,120 --> 01:20:15,120
Well, they would only do it if they expect
economic conditions in the short term, in
the near term are going to deteriorate
materially.

586
01:20:15,120 --> 01:20:31,120
So, causing risk assets like equities to
experience contraction for financial
markets to go down, for earnings of
companies in the stock market to go down
or collapse.

587
01:20:31,120 --> 01:20:55,120
So, an inverted yield curve historically
is a signal, it's a warning sign that the
market is expecting potential economic
slowdown, potential financial markets
collapse from earnings contraction and
potential collapse.

588
01:20:55,120 --> 01:21:08,120
So, we can obviously, again, use this
information as a potential leading
indicator to risk assets, equities, i.e.
the stock market.

589
01:21:08,120 --> 01:21:13,120
Remember guys, we do live in a dynamic
world.

590
01:21:13,120 --> 01:21:29,120
We don't live in a world where, for
example, the yield curve is normal for a
long time and then it suddenly inverts and
there's two polar opposite situations.

591
01:21:29,120 --> 01:21:33,120
We obviously have a period in between.

592
01:21:33,120 --> 01:21:38,120
We have what we call a transition period.

593
01:21:38,120 --> 01:21:41,120
So, this is when yield curves go flat.

594
01:21:41,120 --> 01:21:49,120
So, they can go from being normal,
positive and upward sloping to flat and
then inverting.

595
01:21:49,120 --> 01:21:58,120
Or they can go from inverted to flat and
then normal, positive, upward sloping.

596
01:21:58,120 --> 01:22:10,120
So, we've got some examples here of a flat
yield curve where we're basically at
moments in time in a transitionary period.

597
01:22:10,120 --> 01:22:16,120
So, if we look at the orange line, that is
on the 17th of February 2019.

598
01:22:16,120 --> 01:22:19,120
We know what happened later in 2019.

599
01:22:19,120 --> 01:22:22,120
The yield curve was threatening to invert.

600
01:22:22,120 --> 01:22:28,120
The tens minus twos was threatening to
invert and the Fed took action.

601
01:22:28,120 --> 01:22:37,120
So, back in 2018, when we had a positive
upward sloping curve, the yield curve
began transitioning.

602
01:22:37,120 --> 01:22:45,120
And in early 2019 and going into the
spring of 2019, we essentially had a flat
yield curve.

603
01:22:45,120 --> 01:22:54,120
So, if you look across the maturities, you
can see the Fed fund rates, the official
rate is 2.4.

604
01:22:54,120 --> 01:23:00,120
And you can see across the maturities, one
month money, 2.43.

605
01:23:00,120 --> 01:23:04,120
Same for three-month money, six-month
money, 2.5.

606
01:23:04,120 --> 01:23:07,120
One year, around 2.5, 2.55.

607
01:23:07,120 --> 01:23:13,120
We're around 2.50, 2.55, all the way up to
five-year money.

608
01:23:13,120 --> 01:23:18,120
And then seven-year money and 10-year
money is on a slight premium.

609
01:23:18,120 --> 01:23:27,120
But essentially, the yield curve is flat
and in transition because we know what
happened later in 2019.

610
01:23:27,120 --> 01:23:34,120
The yield curve, the tens minus twos,
almost went below the zero bound.

611
01:23:34,120 --> 01:23:44,120
Then in the summer of 2019, the Fed cut
interest rates, cut nominal rates.

612
01:23:44,120 --> 01:23:46,120
The official rate was going lower.

613
01:23:46,120 --> 01:23:56,120
And by January 2020, where we can see on
the blue line now, we know that the tens
minus twos was positive.

614
01:23:56,120 --> 01:24:09,120
And the yield curve was moving from a
slightly inverted yield curve, but the
tens minus twos not being inverted.

615
01:24:09,120 --> 01:24:11,120
It was short-term rates.

616
01:24:11,120 --> 01:24:17,120
So, one month, two month, and three month
money at a premium to the 10-year.

617
01:24:17,120 --> 01:24:20,120
But two-year money was not at a premium.

618
01:24:20,120 --> 01:24:26,120
So, the tens minus twos was actually
positive by January 2020.

619
01:24:26,120 --> 01:24:38,120
And the yield curve was in transition
going from slightly inverted to flat to
going into positive territory.

620
01:24:38,120 --> 01:24:51,120
And then returning to a more normal
situation of economic expansion and an
upward, positively sloped normal yield
curve.

621
01:24:51,120 --> 01:24:55,120
So, a flat yield curve, how do we read it?

622
01:24:55,120 --> 01:25:06,120
Well, a flat yield curve is characterized
as a potential transitioning period from a
normal yield curve to inverted, an
abnormal yield curve.

623
01:25:06,120 --> 01:25:27,120
So, when the economy is transitioning from
expansion to slowdown, or even contraction
and potential recession, yields on
longer-term bonds tend to fall relative to
shorter-term bonds, which typically rise
on a relative basis.

624
01:25:27,120 --> 01:25:42,120
Investors are engaging in an action of
purchasing long-term bonds relative to
short-term bonds and risk assets like
equities, where they're actually selling.

625
01:25:42,120 --> 01:25:56,120
So, when we're transitioning from
expansion to contraction, we have a flat
yield curve, but it is in the process of
inverting.

626
01:25:56,120 --> 01:26:11,120
When the reverse happens, so when an
economy is expected to come out of
contraction or slowdown and move into
expansion, investors engage in a relative
purchase of short-term bonds.

627
01:26:11,120 --> 01:26:19,120
So, buying short-term bonds and risk
assets like equities and selling long-term
bonds.

628
01:26:19,120 --> 01:26:34,120
So, in that situation, when we're moving
from economic contraction to expansion, we
have a flat yield curve, but it's in the
process of steepening.

629
01:26:34,120 --> 01:26:53,120
So, it's pretty obvious we have to keep an
eye on the yield curve all the time as
traders and portfolio managers, but we
have to understand as well, we are not the
only people in the world looking at the
yield curve.

630
01:26:53,120 --> 01:27:04,120
So, when we see a yield curve
transitioning and going from positive
upward sloping normal to going flat, guess
who else is watching it?

631
01:27:04,120 --> 01:27:05,120
Yes, you guessed it.

632
01:27:05,120 --> 01:27:06,120
Yes, you guessed it.

633
01:27:06,120 --> 01:27:08,120
The Federal Reserve.

634
01:27:08,120 --> 01:27:11,120
The Federal Reserve sees this happening.

635
01:27:11,120 --> 01:27:20,120
And in recent history, when these things
have happened, they are very accommodative
with their policies.

636
01:27:20,120 --> 01:27:30,120
So, in the modern era with these policies,
so, lowering of short-term interest rates
via mechanisms like quantitative easing.

637
01:27:30,120 --> 01:27:34,120
So, buying short-term bonds in the open
market.

638
01:27:34,120 --> 01:27:49,120
As traders, we have to be very careful
because these actions distort the market
price mechanism of the US Treasury market
and all bonds that are priced off the
benchmarks.

639
01:27:49,120 --> 01:27:52,120
The Fed is not stupid.

640
01:27:52,120 --> 01:27:55,120
They see what you see and they act upon
it.

641
01:27:55,120 --> 01:28:12,120
So, for example, in the previous chart
that we looked at, when we looked at the
line for February 2019, the market yield
curve was flat from six months to three
years.

642
01:28:12,120 --> 01:28:16,120
But really, it was one month to three
years.

643
01:28:16,120 --> 01:28:26,120
And in the process of inverting, you
should use the spreadsheet and go back a
few months using the slider and see where
it came from.

644
01:28:26,120 --> 01:28:33,120
But at the time, however, the economy and
the stock market didn't tank in 2019.

645
01:28:33,120 --> 01:28:34,120
Why?

646
01:28:34,120 --> 01:28:38,120
Because the Fed remained incredibly
accommodative.

647
01:28:38,120 --> 01:28:44,120
And in Jan 2020, rates, as you can see,
were a full point lower.

648
01:28:44,120 --> 01:28:55,120
It actually took the exogenous shock of
COVID in 2020 and global economic lockdown
to actually invert the yield curve.

649
01:28:55,120 --> 01:29:04,120
So, have a look at the previous chart when
we're citing February 2020, when the
inversion actually started.

650
01:29:04,120 --> 01:29:31,120
Even then, during that crazy time, the big
sell-off in markets with COVID in 2020,
even then, because we had record levels of
bond purchases from the Fed, the yield
curve actually went back into transition,
was flat, and then actually steepened in
2020 extremely aggressively.

651
01:29:31,120 --> 01:30:00,120
And if we go back and have a look at our
chart that we looked at earlier, where we
plotted 10-year real interest rates, so
nominal adjusted for the consumer price
index, adjusted for inflation, the chart
where we had that plotted against the S&P
500 price, so the level of the S&P 500 and
the price earnings ratio of the S&P,

652
01:30:00,120 --> 01:30:01,120
we can look at that.

653
01:30:01,120 --> 01:30:04,120
We can look at that and just see what
happened in 2020.

654
01:30:04,120 --> 01:30:08,120
We can see that real rates went negative.

655
01:30:08,120 --> 01:30:25,120
And at the same time, if you look at early
2020 on the chart, where real rates were
negative, you can see that the S&P 500
after the COVID sell-off rallied.

656
01:30:25,120 --> 01:30:34,120
And that was also because of additional
major quantitative easing at bond
purchases, suppressing short-term interest
rates in the market.

657
01:30:34,120 --> 01:30:42,120
And then we had big multiple expansion
with the S&P 500 price earnings ratio.

658
01:30:42,120 --> 01:30:53,120
But as we know, the Fed's accommodative
policies were in action in 2019 prior to
COVID.

659
01:30:53,120 --> 01:30:55,120
What was actually happening there?

660
01:30:55,120 --> 01:31:01,120
Well, nominal and real rates began moving
lower in the second quarter of 2019.

661
01:31:01,120 --> 01:31:14,120
The nominal yield curve that we've been
looking at inverted in Q2, and the Fed cut
rates in July, September and October 2019.

662
01:31:14,120 --> 01:31:23,120
The stock market performance in 2019, the
S&P 500 was actually up 30% that year.

663
01:31:23,120 --> 01:31:32,120
The Fed basically remained accommodative
throughout 2019, and the market was
expecting this.

664
01:31:32,120 --> 01:31:35,120
Hence, the move in the yield curve
anticipating it.

665
01:31:35,120 --> 01:31:45,120
So, as a leading indicator, of course, the
yield curve is a good indicator of future
expansion and contraction.

666
01:31:45,120 --> 01:31:57,120
But the Fed also sees this, and in recent
years has become much, much quicker
relative to previous business cycles at
reacting to it.

667
01:31:57,120 --> 01:32:11,120
So much so that it can be said that in the
2009-2020 cycle, a transitioning yield
curve was indicative of the Fed's next
policy move.

668
01:32:11,120 --> 01:32:19,120
Now, if you've ever heard the phrase, the
stock market is not the economy, this is
exactly what it means.

669
01:32:19,120 --> 01:32:26,120
2019 was a good example of this, where
economic slowdown in the background was
occurring.

670
01:32:26,120 --> 01:32:35,120
The signal was being sent via the bond
market, and the Fed could see it.

671
01:32:35,120 --> 01:32:43,120
They were accommodative, they lowered
interest rates, and the stock market was
up 30%.

672
01:32:43,120 --> 01:32:46,120
So, we do have to be careful here, guys.

673
01:32:46,120 --> 01:33:02,120
Just because we see a normal yield curve
going flat and potentially inverting, we
don't want to be in a situation where we
get overly bearish and think everything's
going to collapse overnight.

674
01:33:02,120 --> 01:33:09,120
Because it could be the case that the Fed
is simply going to step in.

675
01:33:09,120 --> 01:33:13,120
So, just take that as a little warning.

676
01:33:13,120 --> 01:33:25,120
We just want to be in a situation all the
time where we're flexible as traders and
understanding that in the real world, you
know, the Fed sees this too.

677
01:33:25,120 --> 01:33:37,120
So, yes, we take this leading indicator as
a useful indicator to let us know what's
going on in the economy.

678
01:33:37,120 --> 01:33:49,120
But we don't automatically think that it
must mean that the stock market has to go
down or even, in the other case, has to go
up.

679
01:33:49,120 --> 01:33:51,120
It's not necessarily the case.

680
01:33:51,120 --> 01:33:54,120
We have to be flexible enough to
understand that.

681
01:33:54,120 --> 01:33:59,120
Now, let's go back to something that we
looked at earlier, where we looked at the
10-year real interest rate.

682
01:33:59,120 --> 01:34:09,120
So, interest rates adjusted for inflation
versus the VIX, the S&P 500 volatility
index.

683
01:34:09,120 --> 01:34:13,120
You can see from the chart, especially in
the last business cycle.

684
01:34:13,120 --> 01:34:22,120
So, when we're looking, say, from the
period 2011 to 2020, what are we looking
at here?

685
01:34:22,120 --> 01:34:38,120
Well, on the right-hand axis, you can see
that real rates are basically 1% or below,
and even at times negative.

686
01:34:38,120 --> 01:34:39,120
So, below zero.

687
01:34:39,120 --> 01:34:41,120
So, real rates being negative.

688
01:34:41,120 --> 01:34:44,120
So, we see this in 2012.

689
01:34:44,120 --> 01:34:47,120
We see it in 2016.

690
01:34:47,120 --> 01:34:54,120
We also see it happening in 2019 and 2020.

691
01:34:54,120 --> 01:34:56,120
So, what is happening here?

692
01:34:56,120 --> 01:35:10,120
Well, when you compare this to the VIX,
which is in green, you can see for pretty
much the entire period, we have suppressed
volatility.

693
01:35:10,120 --> 01:35:17,120
We have volatility trading in the low,
mid, and high teens.

694
01:35:17,120 --> 01:35:22,120
And only on a few occasions does it really
go above 20.

695
01:35:22,120 --> 01:35:39,120
So, for a very, very long period of time
in the last business cycle, so 2011 up
until 2020, before COVID occurred,
volatility in the equities market in the
S&P 500 was low.

696
01:35:39,120 --> 01:35:42,120
It was low and consistently low.

697
01:35:42,120 --> 01:35:43,120
Why?

698
01:35:43,120 --> 01:35:44,120
Why was that?

699
01:35:44,120 --> 01:35:54,120
Well, it comes down to the Fed's
accommodative policy of low interest rates
and quantitative easing measures.

700
01:35:54,120 --> 01:36:11,120
This suppresses short-term rates in the
market, and essentially the Fed was
managing real interest rates to be below
1% and even negative at times.

701
01:36:11,120 --> 01:36:12,120
What is that doing?

702
01:36:12,120 --> 01:36:21,120
Well, it's increasing liquidity in the
financial markets and therefore reducing
volatility.

703
01:36:21,120 --> 01:36:40,120
We learned this earlier that this occurs
relatively when we have periods of, in
history, when we have periods of low real
interest rates, so very low real interest
rates relative to history.

704
01:36:40,120 --> 01:36:48,120
We get low volatility in risk assets
because liquidity is increased.

705
01:36:48,120 --> 01:36:55,120
And in the reverse, if we get high real
interest rates relative to history, what
is happening?

706
01:36:55,120 --> 01:37:02,120
Well, liquidity is going down and then
volatility is increasing.

707
01:37:02,120 --> 01:37:17,120
So, when we're thinking of real interest
rates, so the 10-year real interest rate,
and thinking of the yield curve in the US,
we're not just thinking of it, these
leading indicators.

708
01:37:17,120 --> 01:37:24,120
As indicators, we're not just thinking of
these leading indicators as indicators for
economic growth.

709
01:37:24,120 --> 01:37:39,120
We are also thinking of these leading
indicators in terms of our projection, our
forward-looking expectation of liquidity
and health in the financial markets and
volatility.

710
01:37:40,120 --> 01:37:44,120
So, let's tie all of this up together now.

711
01:37:44,120 --> 01:37:47,120
It's obviously been a very, very long
